<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- This file contains the lecture notes for the section on limits at infinity. -->
  <section xml:id="limits-at-infinity">
    <title>Limits at Infinity</title>
    <p>
      In this section, we will explore limits at infinity, which describe the behavior of a function as the input grows without bound.
    </p>
    <p>
       It may be that the limit does not exist at all! For example, if a function is defined as a ratio like the examples above, the denominator vanishes at our point of interest <m>x=a,</m>
        but unlike those examples the numerator does not vanish at <m>x=a</m> to provide cancellation, then we get an undefined expression, like <m>1/0.</m> 
    </p>
    <p> 
    In such a case, we must examine the values of the functions closely in the neighborhood of
     <m>x=a</m>. If the values ``blow up" to infinity on both sides of <m>x=a</m>,
      we may say that the limit is infinity. Similarly, if the values converge to <m>-\infty</m>
       on both sides, then we may say that the limit is <m>-\infty.</m> 
       But otherwise, all we can say is that the limit is undefined. 
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-infinite-limits">
  <p>
    The following examples demonstrate infinite limits and behavior near a vertical asymptote:
  </p>
  <ol marker="1">
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -1} \frac{x+4}{(x+1)^2} = \infty</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the numerator approaches <m>3</m> and the denominator is always positive and approaches <m>0</m>, the values are positive and become arbitrarily large near <m>x = -1</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -1} \frac{x-2}{(x+1)^2} = -\infty</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the numerator approaches <m>-3</m> and the denominator is always positive and approaches <m>0</m>, all values are negative and large in magnitude near <m>x = -1</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -1} \frac{x-2}{x+1} = \text{undefined}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        The values are negative and large to the right of <m>x = -1</m>, but positive and large on the left side. Because the one-sided limits do not match, the two-sided limit does not exist.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</example>

<assemblage xml:id="asm-limit-infinity">
  <title>Limit at Infinity Rule</title>
  <p>
    <me>\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{1}{x} = 0</me>
  </p>
</assemblage>

<example xml:id="ex-limits-infinity-list">
  <title>Limits at Infinity</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Find the following limits:</p>
    <ol marker="1">
      <li>
        <p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \infty}\left(1+\frac{1}{x}\right)</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -\infty}\frac{\sqrt{2}}{x^2}</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \infty}\sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \infty}x\sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)</m></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <ol marker="1">
      <li>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to \infty</m>, the fraction <m>\frac{1}{x} \to 0</m>. 
          Thus, <m>1 + 0 = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to -\infty</m>, <m>x^2 \to \infty</m>. 
          Dividing a constant by a large number gives <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Substituting <m>\theta = \frac{1}{x}</m>, as <m>x \to \infty</m>, <m>\theta \to 0</m>.
          Since <m>\sin(0) = 0</m>, the limit is <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Rewriting as <m>\lim_{\theta \to 0} \frac{\sin \theta}{\theta}</m> where <m>\theta = \frac{1}{x}</m>.
          This is a fundamental limit equal to <m>1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-infinite-limit-rational">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2} \frac{1}{(x-2)^2}</m>, if it exists.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      We analyze the behavior of the function as <m>x</m> approaches <m>2</m> from both sides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to 2^-</m>, the term <m>x-2</m> is negative but very small. Squaring it makes <m>(x-2)^2</m> a very small positive number. Therefore, <m>\frac{1}{(x-2)^2} \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to 2^+</m>, the term <m>x-2</m> is positive and very small. Squaring it again results in a very small positive number. Therefore, <m>\frac{1}{(x-2)^2} \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Since the function increases without bound from both sides, we write:
      <me>
        \lim_{x \to 2} \frac{1}{(x-2)^2} = \infty
      </me>
      The limit does not exist as a finite real number, but it is described as being infinite.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^-} \frac{1}{(x-1)^3}</m>, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^+} \frac{1}{(x-1)^3}</m>, and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1} \frac{1}{(x-1)^3}</m>, if they exist.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      We examine the behavior of the function <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{(x-1)^3}</m> near <m>x = 1</m>:
    </p>
    <ol marker="1">
      <li>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to 1^-</m>, <m>x-1</m> is a small negative number. Cubing a negative number keeps it negative, so <m>(x-1)^3</m> is a very small negative value. Therefore:
          <me>\lim_{x \to 1^-} \frac{1}{(x-1)^3} = -\infty</me>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to 1^+</m>, <m>x-1</m> is a small positive number. Cubing it results in a very small positive value. Therefore:
          <me>\lim_{x \to 1^+} \frac{1}{(x-1)^3} = \infty</me>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Because the left-hand limit (<m>-\infty</m>) and the right-hand limit (<m>\infty</m>) are not the same, the two-sided limit does not exist:
          <me>\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{1}{(x-1)^3} = \text{DNE}</me>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<theorem xml:id="thm-limits-at-infinity-power">
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>k</m> is a positive rational number and <m>c</m> is any real number, then
      <me>
        \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{c}{x^k} = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{c}{x^k} = 0
      </me>
      provided that <m>x^k</m> is defined for all required values of <m>x</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="ex-limit-rational-infinity">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>f(x) = \displaystyle\frac{3x^2+2x+1}{2x^2+5}</m>. Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \infty} f(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      To calculate this limit, we first find the highest power of <m>x</m> that appears in the function. In this case, it is <m>2</m>, showing up in both the numerator and denominator. Next, we divide every term by <m>x</m> raised to this highest power. In this case, we divide every term by <m>x^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We get:
      <md>
        <mrow>\lim_{x\to \infty} \frac{3x^2+2x+1}{2x^2+5} \amp = \lim_{x\to \infty} \frac{\frac{3x^2}{x^2} + \frac{2x}{x^2} + \frac{1}{x^2}}{\frac{2x^2}{x^2} + \frac{5}{x^2}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x\to \infty} \frac{3 + \frac{2}{x} + \frac{1}{x^2}}{2 + \frac{5}{x^2}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \frac{3 + 0 + 0}{2 + 0}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \frac{3}{2}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Evaluate the following limits:
    </p>
    <ol marker="1">
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \rightarrow -\infty}\frac{2x^2-5}{3x^2+x+2}</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \rightarrow -\infty}\frac{2x-5}{3x^4+x+2}</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \rightarrow -\infty}\frac{2x^5-5}{3x^2+x+2}</m>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <ol marker="1">
      <li>
        <p>
          Divide the numerator and denominator by <m>x^2</m>:
          <me>\lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{2 - 5/x^2}{3 + 1/x + 2/x^2} = \frac{2-0}{3+0+0} = \frac{2}{3}</me>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Divide by the highest power <m>x^4</m>:
          <me>\lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{2/x^3 - 5/x^4}{3 + 1/x^3 + 2/x^4} = \frac{0-0}{3+0+0} = 0</me>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Divide by <m>x^2</m>:
          <me>\lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{2x^3 - 5/x^2}{3 + 1/x + 2/x^2}</me>
          Since the numerator behaves like <m>2x^3</m> and <m>x \to -\infty</m>, the limit is <m>-\infty</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<assemblage xml:id="asm-rational-limit-rules">
  <title>Summary of Limits of Rational Functions at Infinity</title>
  <p>
    Let <m>p(x)</m> and <m>q(x)</m> be polynomial functions. To find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to \pm\infty} \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>, compare the degree of the numerator (<m>n</m>) and the degree of the denominator (<m>d</m>):
  </p>
  <p>
    <ul marker="disc">
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>If <m>n \lt d</m>:</strong> The limit is always <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>If <m>n = d</m>:</strong> The limit is the ratio of the leading coefficients.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>If <m>n \gt d</m>:</strong> The limit is <m>\infty</m> or <m>-\infty</m> (determine the sign by checking the leading terms).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</assemblage>

<exercise xml:id="ex-limit-radical-infinity">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x)</m> and <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to \infty} f(x)</m> if <m>f(x) = \displaystyle\frac{\sqrt{9x^2+2}}{4x+3}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      To evaluate these limits, we divide the numerator and denominator by <m>|x| = \sqrt{x^2}</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      For <m>x \to \infty</m>, we have <m>x = \sqrt{x^2}</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\sqrt{9x^2+2}}{4x+3} \amp = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\sqrt{\frac{9x^2}{x^2} + \frac{2}{x^2}}}{\frac{4x}{x} + \frac{3}{x}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \frac{\sqrt{9+0}}{4+0} = \frac{3}{4}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      For <m>x \to -\infty</m>, we have <m>x = -\sqrt{x^2}</m> (or <m>\sqrt{x^2} = -x</m>). This introduces a negative sign when we divide the denominator by the radical:
      <md>
        <mrow>\lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{\sqrt{9x^2+2}}{4x+3} \amp = \lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{\sqrt{9 + \frac{2}{x^2}}}{x\left(4 + \frac{3}{x}\right) \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2}}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{\sqrt{9 + \frac{2}{x^2}}}{-\left(4 + \frac{3}{x}\right)}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \frac{3}{-4} = -\frac{3}{4}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to \infty} \left(\sqrt{x^2+x} - \sqrt{x^2-x}\right)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      This limit has the indeterminate form <m>\infty - \infty</m>. To evaluate it, we rationalize the expression by multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate:
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{(\sqrt{x^2+x} - \sqrt{x^2-x})(\sqrt{x^2+x} + \sqrt{x^2-x})}{\sqrt{x^2+x} + \sqrt{x^2-x}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{(x^2+x) - (x^2-x)}{\sqrt{x^2+x} + \sqrt{x^2-x}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{2x}{\sqrt{x^2+x} + \sqrt{x^2-x}}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, we divide the numerator and denominator by <m>x</m> (noting that <m>x = \sqrt{x^2}</m> for <m>x \gt 0</m>):
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{2}{\frac{\sqrt{x^2+x}}{\sqrt{x^2}} + \frac{\sqrt{x^2-x}}{\sqrt{x^2}}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{2}{\sqrt{1 + 1/x} + \sqrt{1 - 1/x}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \frac{2}{\sqrt{1+0} + \sqrt{1-0}} = \frac{2}{2} = 1</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
    </section>